---
title: "API Gateway"
category: "IoT & Edge"
difficulty: "Medium"
tags: ["api-gateway", "edge", "envoy", "rate-limiting", "circuit-breaking", "grpc", "transcoding"]
---

## Overview

This system is a unified entry point for IoT and edge traffic that enforces policy (routing, quotas, and safety) while translating client-friendly JSON/HTTP into internal gRPC. The key insight is to treat the gateway as a **fast, dumb data plane** (packet-level decisions, no business logic) driven by a **small control plane** (policy + config distribution). That split keeps latency low at the edge and keeps policy changes safe and auditable.

Elegance comes from solving the one hard problem—**enforcing multi-tenant limits and resilience without turning the gateway into a centralized bottleneck**—with a two-level approach: local, deterministic enforcement on each gateway instance plus a lightweight global limiter for fairness across the fleet. Everything else stays boring: Envoy for the data plane, protobuf as the source of truth, and standard observability primitives.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive gateways centralize every decision (rate limit checks, auth introspection, routing lookups). At IoT scale, that becomes self-inflicted DDoS: the gateway collapses precisely when traffic spikes or backends degrade. The trap is “strong global consistency for limits” — it looks correct on paper and fails in production.

The second trap is protocol translation without a schema contract. “Just map JSON to gRPC” turns into silent breaking changes, inconsistent error models, and impossible debugging across device firmware versions. Translation must be anchored on a single canonical contract and versioning strategy.

